WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:02.850
What does it mean to be an African woman?

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:04.679
Well, many things.

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:13.838
For the Sapeuses of Brazzaville, Congo, it means dressing up in fabulous fashions,

00:00:14.192 --> 00:00:20.467
taking on an alter ego that challenges gender conventions and redefines their role in society.

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:25.472
I've spent years documenting the experiences of women across Africa,

00:00:26.004 --> 00:00:32.548
focusing on themes like our education, sexuality, health, creativity, and self-empowerment.

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:37.239
Messani Grace is the leader of Mavula Sape.

00:00:37.461 --> 00:00:44.013
She's been a Sapeuse for the last three decades, well before women were allowed to join official groups.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:44.780
Hello!

00:00:45.442 --> 00:00:45.939
Hi!

00:00:45.970 --> 00:00:47.069
Thank you for having me.

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:50.351
Mama Grace, I'm curious, how did the movement start?

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:57.072
The story of La Sape here in Congo started at the end of the Second World War.

00:00:57.294 --> 00:01:01.590
There were the veterans who left for the war over there.

00:01:02.031 --> 00:01:06.327
They could see how the French dressed, in culottes, shirts, shoes, colonial helmet.

00:01:06.659 --> 00:01:08.149
They copied the style from over there.

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:14.258
I find it interesting that part of the movement was taken from the colonial style of dress

00:01:14.372 --> 00:01:21.499
but along the way, people started to own it for themselves and said -- this is our culture, this is our identity.

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:24.810
Initially there were no Sapeuses, there were only men.

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:29.685
When I started dressing in a suit and tie, in the beginning even I felt embarrassed.

00:01:30.348 --> 00:01:32.876
The world of La Sape makes me feel valued.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:38.830
It is a world of respect in places where others cannot easily access it.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:43.568
You are also becoming a Sapeuse.

00:01:44.095 --> 00:01:45.440
I love it!

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:49.890
La Sape represents more than just luxury clothing.

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:51.510
It's become a way of life.

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:59.973
When I see my mum dress up, it also makes me want to dress like that.

00:02:00.565 --> 00:02:04.258
It's rare to see girls at my age become Sapeuses.

00:02:04.259 --> 00:02:09.081
I am no longer embarrassed. I feel confident to express myself like this.

00:02:10.691 --> 00:02:11.220
Okay.

00:02:11.710 --> 00:02:17.490
I love how the women have formed collectives to differentiate themselves and express solidarity.

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:21.617
This is how they take control of their own identities

00:02:21.716 --> 00:02:26.144
and provide a platform and possibilities for other women to do the same.

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:34.865
Female creativity continues to grow across Africa as more and more women take charge,

00:02:34.865 --> 00:02:40.190
refashioning their roles in society and turning ideas into enterprises.

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:46.889
Gambia is known as the smiling coast of Africa.

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:50.969
It is the country that inspires my work.

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:56.129
I transform everyday objects into African inspired products.

00:02:57.160 --> 00:03:00.459
When I have an idea, I talk it through with Kaddija.

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:03.849
She understands my work better than anybody else.

00:03:04.570 --> 00:03:11.020
So sometimes I tell her what I want and she throws in her own ideas too.

00:03:11.291 --> 00:03:16.804
DHL services came at a time when we were struggling to send our products abroad to customers

00:03:16.903 --> 00:03:18.734
as normal process could take months.

00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:22.778
My creations make me feel proud to be a Gambian

00:03:22.975 --> 00:03:25.054
and I just want to show Africa to the world.

